/**
	A pack file stores many files inside one larger file, usually so that a game's sounds and other assets can be distributed together and optionally encrypted.
	pack_file();
	## Remarks:
		A pack_file object is inactive when created. Call `create()` to start writing a new pack, or `open()` to read an existing one. A pack is either being written or being read, never both: you cannot add files to a pack that was opened for reading, and you cannot read files out of a pack until it has been written, closed, and opened again.
		Packs can be encrypted by passing a key to `create()`. The same key must then be passed to `open()`, otherwise opening fails.
		Packs can also be embedded into your compiled game with the `#pragma embed packname.dat` directive. To open an embedded pack, put an asterisk before its name, for example `open("*packname.dat")`. For BGT compatibility, opening a pack called `*` opens the first embedded pack. This also works when you run the script from source, in which case the pack is read from disk instead.
		Functions that load assets, such as `sound::load()`, accept a `pack_interface` handle. A pack_file converts to a pack_interface implicitly, so you can pass one directly or assign it to `sound_default_pack`. To get the pack_file back from a pack_interface handle, use `cast<pack_file@>()`.
		Files inside a pack are identified by name. Names are case sensitive, must be valid UTF-8, and must be unique within the pack.
		This class replaces the older `pack` class, which is now only available through the legacy_sound plugin. The two classes use different file formats.
*/

// Example:
void main() {
	pack_file p;
	if (!p.create("example.dat")) {
		alert("Error", "Unable to create the pack.");
		return;
	}
	p.add_memory("greeting.txt", "Hello from inside a pack!");
	p.close();
	if (!p.open("example.dat")) {
		alert("Error", "Unable to open the pack.");
		return;
	}
	datastream@ ds = p.get_file("greeting.txt");
	alert("greeting.txt", ds.read());
	ds.close(); // The pack file can't be deleted while a stream still has it open.
	p.close();
	file_delete("example.dat");
}
